{"version":"6.9","hitCount":1,"request":{"queryString":"EXT_ID:21956331 AND SRC:MED","internalQuery":"EXT_ID:21956331 AND SRC:MED","resultType":"CORE","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":25,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"21956331","source":"MED","pmid":"21956331","pmcid":"PMC3448949","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC3448949"]},"doi":"10.1038/nature10488","title":"Conformational changes in the G protein Gs induced by the β2 adrenergic receptor.","authorString":"Chung KY, Rasmussen SG, Liu T, Li S, DeVree BT, Chae PS, Calinski D, Kobilka BK, Woods VL, Sunahara RK.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Chung KY","lastName":"Chung","firstName":"Ka Young","initials":"KY","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9038-9571"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Molecular and Cellular Physiology, Stanford University School of Medicine, Stanford, California 94305, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Rasmussen SG","lastName":"Rasmussen","firstName":"Søren G F","initials":"SG","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1853-1841"}},{"fullName":"Liu T","lastName":"Liu","firstName":"Tong","initials":"T"},{"fullName":"Li S","lastName":"Li","firstName":"Sheng","initials":"S"},{"fullName":"DeVree BT","lastName":"DeVree","firstName":"Brian T","initials":"BT","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0582-8125"}},{"fullName":"Chae PS","lastName":"Chae","firstName":"Pil Seok","initials":"PS"},{"fullName":"Calinski D","lastName":"Calinski","firstName":"Diane","initials":"D"},{"fullName":"Kobilka BK","lastName":"Kobilka","firstName":"Brian K","initials":"BK","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5958-3990"}},{"fullName":"Woods VL Jr","lastName":"Woods","firstName":"Virgil L","initials":"VL"},{"fullName":"Sunahara RK","lastName":"Sunahara","firstName":"Roger K","initials":"RK","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1702-8619"}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5958-3990"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9038-9571"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1853-1841"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0582-8125"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1702-8619"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data","recommended_reviews","related_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"7366","volume":"477","journalIssueId":1867788,"dateOfPublication":"2011 Sep","monthOfPublication":9,"yearOfPublication":2011,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Nature","title":"Nature","essn":"1476-4687","issn":"0028-0836","nlmid":"0410462","isoabbreviation":"Nature"},"printPublicationDate":"2011-09-01"},"pubYear":"2011","pageInfo":"611-615","abstractText":"G protein-coupled receptors represent the largest family of membrane receptors that instigate signalling through nucleotide exchange on heterotrimeric G proteins. Nucleotide exchange, or more precisely, GDP dissociation from the G protein α-subunit, is the key step towards G protein activation and initiation of downstream signalling cascades. Despite a wealth of biochemical and biophysical studies on inactive and active conformations of several heterotrimeric G proteins, the molecular underpinnings of G protein activation remain elusive. To characterize this mechanism, we applied peptide amide hydrogen-deuterium exchange mass spectrometry to probe changes in the structure of the heterotrimeric bovine G protein, Gs (the stimulatory G protein for adenylyl cyclase) on formation of a complex with agonist-bound human β(2) adrenergic receptor (β(2)AR). Here we report structural links between the receptor-binding surface and the nucleotide-binding pocket of Gs that undergo higher levels of hydrogen-deuterium exchange than would be predicted from the crystal structure of the β(2)AR-Gs complex. Together with X-ray crystallographic and electron microscopic data of the β(2)AR-Gs complex (from refs 2, 3), we provide a rationale for a mechanism of nucleotide exchange, whereby the receptor perturbs the structure of the amino-terminal region of the α-subunit of Gs and consequently alters the 'P-loop' that binds the β-phosphate in GDP. As with the Ras family of small-molecular-weight G proteins, P-loop stabilization and β-phosphate coordination are key determinants of GDP (and GTP) binding affinity.","affiliation":"Department of Molecular and Cellular Physiology, Stanford University School of Medicine, Stanford, California 94305, USA.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","research-article","Journal Article","Research Support, N.I.H., Extramural"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"NCI NIH HHS","acronym":"CA","grantId":"CA118595","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIGMS NIH HHS","acronym":"GM","grantId":"GM068603","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIAID NIH HHS","acronym":"AI","grantId":"R01 AI081982","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NHLBI NIH HHS","acronym":"HL","grantId":"HL071078","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NINDS NIH HHS","acronym":"NS","grantId":"NS28471","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIAID NIH HHS","acronym":"AI","grantId":"R01 AI072106","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIAID NIH HHS","acronym":"AI","grantId":"AI076961","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIGMS NIH HHS","acronym":"GM","grantId":"GM066170","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIDDK NIH HHS","acronym":"DK","grantId":"P60 DK020572","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIGMS NIH HHS","acronym":"GM","grantId":"R01 GM066170","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIGMS NIH HHS","acronym":"GM","grantId":"R01 GM020501","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Lundbeck Foundation","grantId":"R37-2009-3457","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIAID NIH HHS","acronym":"AI","grantId":"AI081982","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIGMS NIH HHS","acronym":"GM","grantId":"GM008270","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIGMS NIH HHS","acronym":"GM","grantId":"GM093325","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIGMS NIH HHS","acronym":"GM","grantId":"R01 GM068603","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIGMS NIH HHS","acronym":"GM","grantId":"R01 GM083118","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIGMS NIH HHS","acronym":"GM","g